# Welcome page: the page the window opens on. It says what the toolkit is for,
# what to do first, and which page answers which question.
#
# "Language" means two different things in this application: the language of
# this window, and the languages the camera's own menu offers. They get a
# heading each rather than a clause each, because confusing the two is the
# mistake this page exists to prevent.
nav-welcome = Welcome
welcome-intro-heading = What this program does
welcome-intro-body = This program keeps Sony cameras usable. Since Sony shut down its store for PlayMemories Camera Apps, purchased apps can neither be backed up nor reinstalled there, and resetting the camera deletes them for good. With this program you back up installed apps and put them back, and you install .apk files of your own or apps from sources you add yourself. It also switches on settings the camera has but does not offer in its menu, and saves the camera's settings memory. It talks to the camera directly over the USB cable.
welcome-start-heading = The first step
welcome-start-body = Switch the camera on and, while the cable is still out, set USB connection in its menu to “Auto”; once the cable is in, the menu can no longer be reached. Then connect the camera by USB, open Camera info and press “Get camera info”. Once the model and the firmware version appear, the connection is sound. The same page shows what your model supports; most features also need the key material.
welcome-start-driver = On Windows, tweaks, backups, the service shell and the function analysis need a driver of this program's own for the camera while the session lasts. Windows lets only administrators assign a driver, so the program then offers to restart with administrator rights, and Windows asks you to confirm. When the session ends, the assignment is undone. Camera info, apps, GPS and firmware updates need none of this.
welcome-start-driver-linux = On Linux nothing has to be replaced: the camera is reached directly. What is needed once is the right to open the device — a udev rule for Sony's vendor id — and, for mass-storage mode, releasing the kernel driver usb-storage. Both are prepared outside this program with root rights; the script scripts/linux-prepare.sh in the source tree does both. This window itself never asks for such rights.
welcome-start-driver-macos = On macOS nothing has to be installed or replaced. For the tweaks, the service shell, the function analysis, firmware updates and the updater shell this window asks once for your administrator password: they go through the camera's mass-storage connection, which macOS hands only to such a program. macOS 26 currently hands it to no program at all; there, for now only the functions that run over MTP work, such as Camera info.
welcome-privacy-heading = Your data
welcome-privacy-where = The full privacy notice is on the License page, together with the licences of every library this program is built from.
welcome-where-heading = Where to find what
welcome-where-info = Model, serial number, firmware version and what the model supports, plus the shutter count. This is also the connection test.
welcome-where-compat = Which camera models support apps, the updater shell and the service shell. Needs no camera.
welcome-where-install = Camera apps from the sources you have added, or an .apk file of your own; and a backup of the apps installed on the camera.
welcome-where-tweaks = The settings the camera's menu does not reach, the languages among them.
welcome-where-backup = Save the camera's whole settings memory to a file, and write it back if a change goes wrong.
welcome-where-compare = Read the settings memory of several cameras or load it from backups, and lay two or more side by side.
welcome-where-network = The access points and services used for live streaming.
welcome-where-gps = Fresh GPS assist data for the camera, so it finds its position faster.
welcome-where-analysis = Find out in which value of its settings memory the camera keeps one of its menu settings.
welcome-where-firmware = Write a firmware image to the camera.
welcome-where-tools = Unpack and inspect firmware and package files. Needs no camera.
welcome-where-shell = The service shell and the updater shell: direct access to the camera's memory and filesystem. Switch on the advanced mode in Settings first.
welcome-where-settings = Editing the translation of this program, app and plugin sources, plugins, the update notification, the advanced mode and the function analysis.
welcome-where-license = Where this port came from, whose work it builds on, and the terms it is under.
welcome-where-hint = No page acts on the camera by itself. Nothing is read or written until you press a button that says it will.
nav-diagnose = Diagnosis
diagnose-hint = Help when something does not work. Everything on this page only reads: nothing is changed on the camera, and on this computer only the files you choose to save are written.
welcome-where-diagnose = Read-only checks for when something does not work, including the camera's internal usage log, read and shown here.
welcome-compat-heading = Is my camera supported?
welcome-compat-info = Camera info works with most Sony cameras that connect over USB.
welcome-compat-apps = Apps run only on models with the PlayMemories Camera Apps platform, which only older models have.
welcome-compat-service = Tweaks, the shells and firmware updates depend on the camera's processor. The list comes from the original project and from community reports. Before you install apps, GPS data or firmware, or back up apps, the program tells you what the list knows about your model.
btn-open-compat = Open the compatibility list
welcome-net-intro = It connects to the network only for the following, and the other end learns your IP address in the process:
welcome-net-github = GitHub: reading the sources you have added and their stars, searching for sources and projects, refreshing pinned projects — each only when you click.
welcome-net-downloads = The addresses those sources name, when you download an app or a plugin.
welcome-net-gps = Sony, when you download GPS assist data.
welcome-net-keys = The address you enter when you import key material.
welcome-net-update = GitHub at every start if you have switched on the update notification, and once when you click the version number.
