reference-title = Check a known finding
reference-intro = Enter where a setting sits (a property id) and the values it can take — typed in or imported from a shared analysis report — and the program reads just those properties from your camera to see whether it stores the same thing in the same place. It only reads, unless you ask for the write test.
reference-own-hardware = Use your own camera only.
reference-help-toggle = How it works and why it is low-risk
reference-help-how-heading = How it works
reference-help-how = The easiest way is to import a shared analysis report from another camera: it fills in the findings for you. You can also enter them by hand — a property id and the values it is said to take. Either way, the program reads exactly those properties from your own camera and reports whether each one exists, its current value, and which of your values it matches. Nothing is written unless you start the optional write test.
reference-help-safety-heading = Safety
reference-help-safety-intro = The write test is optional and built to keep the risk of bricking the camera as low as possible:
reference-help-safe-own = It only ever touches your own, connected camera, checked by model and serial number before every write.
reference-help-safe-optin = Reading is the default; the write test is opt-in and asks for confirmation twice.
reference-help-safe-rollback = The original value is saved before the write, so an interrupted test can be undone at the next start.
reference-help-safe-guard = After the write the value is read back and the write is verified.
reference-help-safe-small = Only small values are written (up to 16 bytes, never ones the camera marks read-only or protected).
reference-help-safe-noflash = It writes a single setting value, not firmware — there is no firmware flash involved.
reference-start-heading = Findings to check
reference-id-hint = The property id is an eight-digit hex number, e.g. 0x01640001. Each value is the stored bytes as hex, e.g. 00 or 01 02.
