compare-hint = Reads the settings memory of several cameras and lays the results side by side. A camera is only read from, never written to.
compare-explanation-toggle = Explanation: purpose, procedure, risks
compare-purpose-heading = What for?
compare-purpose = The comparison makes visible where cameras differ in their settings memory. That is where everything the camera permanently knows about itself is kept, including settings its menu never shows. Bodies of the same model from different sales regions also differ in individual values there. The comparison finds such places; what they mean still has to be worked out by someone.
compare-how-heading = How it goes
compare-how = Each camera is read once and the result saved as a dump: a readable JSON file. With two or more of them the comparison lays them side by side and sorts every property into one finding: the same everywhere, nearly the same, different, stored at a different length, apparently unused, missing on one camera, or changing by itself. The result can be saved with any selection of those findings.
compare-reads-heading = What is read
compare-reads = Reading a camera needs service mode and, on Windows and macOS, administrator rights; on Windows the program exchanges the USB driver for the duration. It only ever reads and changes nothing on the camera. The comparison itself works on files and touches no camera at all.
compare-limits-heading = Limits
compare-limits = The findings describe bytes, not what they mean; what apparently unused and missing stand for is explained with the findings. Unclaimed bytes in the image are not room for new features: a value takes effect only where the firmware asks for it.
compare-risk-heading = Risks and data
compare-risk = A dump is not a backup. It cannot be written back to the camera; only a backup image can. Anyone who wants a way back makes one on the page named below. A dump holds every value of the settings memory; the serial number is left out unless you ask for it. These files are meant to be passed around, so look at what is in one before sharing it.
compare-count-label = How many cameras do you want to compare? You can add another one at any time (up to { $max }).
compare-list-heading = Your dumps
compare-label-field = Name for this camera
compare-label-hint = Freely chosen and kept inside this program. It becomes the heading of this camera's column, so a name you recognise is worth more than the model number.
