compare-distance-heading = How near is nearly the same?
compare-distance-help = Two values count as nearly the same when they differ in no more than this many bytes. One is the sensible default: a setting stored as a small number differs in its last byte and nowhere else. Zero means only identical values count as equal. Higher numbers group more together, and produce more coincidences.
compare-noise-heading = What is noise?
compare-noise-help = Some values the camera changes by itself — shutter counts, the clock, hours in use. Give it two dumps of the same camera and whatever differs between them can only have come from the camera. Those values are then marked as changing by itself instead of as a difference between cameras. Without such a pair nothing happens.
compare-findings-toggle = What do the findings mean?
compare-class-identical-help = Every camera holds the same value here. Most of the settings memory looks like this.
compare-class-similar-help = The values differ in very few bytes — usually a single one. This is where a setting stored as a small number shows up.
compare-class-different-help = The values differ in more than a few bytes. Often text, a date or a counter rather than a setting.
compare-class-structural-help = The property has different lengths on different cameras, so the values cannot sensibly be held side by side. Usually a sign that the models store it differently.
compare-class-empty-help = A judgement, not a reading: the value is all zeros or all ones, matches the factory value and reads the same on every camera. The format has no mark for an unused property, and zero is also how most settings spell off.
compare-class-absent-help = A fact: at least one camera has no entry for this property, so there are no bytes for it either. That is not the same as a property that exists and holds nothing.
compare-class-noise-help = Known to change without anybody changing it. Only recognised when two dumps come from the same camera; see the note on noise above.
compare-shape-heading = Looks like a setting
compare-shape-help = A mark, not a finding: it describes the form of a value rather than the outcome of the comparison. Switch means one byte that reads 00 or 01, that the cameras disagree about, and that can be written. Short list means the same but with any value up to 0f, which is how a choice of a few options looks. Both only narrow the search down. Not every byte of 00 or 01 is a setting — the firmware keeps status flags the same way — and a setting stored as a single bit inside a byte will never show up here.
